'''LeBron Raymone James Sr.''' ({{IPAc-en|l|ə|ˈ|b|r|ɒ|n}} {{respelling|lə|BRON}};<ref>{{Cite encyclopedia |title=James, Lebron |encyclopedia=[[Longman Dictionary of Contemporary English]] |url=https://www.ldoceonline.com/dictionary/lebron-james |access-date=June 24, 2026 |via=LDOCE Online}}</ref> born December 30, 1984) is an American professional [[basketball]] player for the [[Los Angeles Lakers]] of the [[National Basketball Association]] (NBA). Nicknamed "'''King James'''", he is the [[List of NBA career scoring leaders|NBA's all-time leading scorer]] and has won four [[List of NBA champions|NBA championships]] from 10 [[NBA Finals]] appearances, including eight consecutive appearances between 2011 and 2018.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|title=LeBron James Bio|url=https://www.nba.com/player/2544/lebron-james/bio|website=NBA.com|date=January 1, 2021|access-date=April 15, 2022|archive-date=April 15, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220415214923/https://www.nba.com/player/2544/lebron-james/bio|url-status=live }}</ref> He has also won three [[Basketball at the Summer Olympics|Olympic gold medals]] as a member of the [[United States men's national basketball team|U.S. national team]]. James is widely considered to be one of the greatest basketball players of all time and one of the greatest athletes in modern history.<ref>{{cite web |title=Top 100 Athletes of the 21st Century |url=https://www.espn.co.uk/espn/story/_/id/40446224/top-100-athletes-21st-century |website=ESPN}}</ref> <ref>{{Cite news |last=Staff |first=The Athletic NBA |date=2025-06-10 |title=NBA 75: Top 75 NBA players of all time, from MJ and LeBron to Lenny Wilkens |url=https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/3137873/2022/02/23/the-nba-75-the-top-75-nba-players-of-all-time-from-mj-and-lebron-to-lenny-wilkens/ |access-date=2025-06-23 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331 |archive-date=April 7, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220407031715/https://theathletic.com/3137873/2022/02/23/the-nba-75-the-top-75-nba-players-of-all-time-from-mj-and-lebron-to-lenny-wilkens/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url =https://www.si.com/nba/2016/02/08/michael-jordan-lebron-james-stephen-curry-nba-greatest#gid=ci02556958e0022580&pid=50--paul-arizin |title =SI's 50 greatest players in NBA history |author =Jack McCallum |date =February 8, 2016 |publisher =[[Sports illustrated]] |accessdate =2024-03-29 |archiveurl =https://web.archive.org/web/20240329124115/https://www.si.com/nba/2016/02/08/michael-jordan-lebron-james-stephen-curry-nba-greatest#gid=ci02556958e0022580&pid=50--paul-arizin |archivedate =2024-03-29}}</ref>

In addition to ranking [[List of NBA career assists leaders|fourth in NBA career assists]] and [[List of NBA career steals leaders|sixth in NBA career steals]], James holds several individual honors, including four [[NBA MVP]] awards, four [[NBA Finals Most Valuable Player|Finals MVP]] awards, the [[NBA Rookie of the Year|Rookie of the Year]] award, three [[NBA All-Star Game Kobe Bryant Most Valuable Player Award|All-Star Game MVP]] awards, the inaugural [[NBA Cup]] MVP, and the [[FIBA Men's Olympics Most Valuable Player|Olympics MVP]] in the [[Basketball at the 2024 Summer Olympics – Men's tournament|2024 Summer Olympics]]. A record 22-time [[NBA All-Star|All-Star]] and 21-time [[All-NBA Team|All-NBA selection]] (including a record 13 First Team selections), he has also made six [[NBA All-Defensive First Team|All-Defensive Teams]].<!-- Please do not add "first team All-Rookie" as this is a minor achievement in the grand scheme of LeBron's accomplishments. It does not need to be in the lead.--> The [[List of oldest and youngest NBA players|oldest active player in the NBA]], he holds records for the [[List of NBA seasons played leaders|most seasons played]] with 23, the [[List of NBA career games played leaders|most games played]], the [[List of NBA career minutes played leaders|most minutes played]], and the most field goals made in league history.

Born and raised in [[Akron, Ohio]], James gained national attention at [[St. Vincent–St. Mary High School]] and was heavily touted as a future NBA superstar for his all-around scoring, passing, athleticism and playmaking abilities.<ref>{{cite news |last=Wahl |first=Grant |date=February 18, 2002 |title=Ohio High School Junior LeBron James Is So Good That He's Already Being Mentioned as the Heir to Air Jordan |url=https://vault.si.com/vault/2002/02/18/ahead-of-his-class-ohio-high-school-junior-lebron-james-is-so-good-that-hes-already-being-mentioned-as-the-heir-to-air-jordan |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230702103936/https://vault.si.com/vault/2002/02/18/ahead-of-his-class-ohio-high-school-junior-lebron-james-is-so-good-that-hes-already-being-mentioned-as-the-heir-to-air-jordan |archive-date=July 2, 2023 |access-date=July 1, 2023 |newspaper=Sports Illustrated Vault &#124; SI.com}}</ref> A [[prep-to-pro]], the [[Cleveland Cavaliers]] selected James with the [[List of first overall NBA draft picks|first overall pick]] of the [[2003 NBA draft]]. He won Rookie of the Year and quickly established himself as one of the league's premier players, leading Cleveland to its first NBA Finals appearance in [[2007 NBA Finals|2007]] and winning the [[List of National Basketball Association annual scoring leaders|scoring title]] in 2008. After winning back-to-back [[Most valuable player|MVPs]] in 2009 and 2010, he joined the [[Miami Heat]] as a [[Free agent (NBA)|free agent]] in 2010, a controversial move announced in the television special ''[[The Decision (TV program)|The Decision]]''.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|first=Tom|last=Withers|title=AP Was There: LeBron James dumps Cleveland for Miami|url=https://apnews.com/article/miami-heat-cleveland-cavaliers-nba-entertainment-sports-c842cbbffbd42a55ed8374fd1e1b4a93|work=AP News|date=April 6, 2022|access-date=April 15, 2022|archive-date=April 15, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220415214923/https://apnews.com/article/miami-heat-cleveland-cavaliers-nba-entertainment-sports-c842cbbffbd42a55ed8374fd1e1b4a93|url-status=live}}</ref>

With the Heat, James won his first two NBA championships in [[2012 NBA Finals|2012]] and [[2013 NBA Finals|2013]], earning MVP and Finals MVP honors both years. After four seasons in Miami, he returned to Cleveland in 2014, leading the Cavaliers to their first-ever championship in [[2016 NBA Finals|2016]], ending the [[Cleveland sports curse]]. He signed with the Lakers in 2018, winning another title in [[2020 NBA Finals|2020]] and becoming the first player to win Finals MVP with three different teams. Off the court, James has earned further wealth and fame from numerous endorsement contracts. He is the first player in NBA history to accumulate $1&nbsp;billion in earnings as an active player.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|first=Jasmyn|last=Wimbish|title=LeBron James becomes first player in NBA history to make $1&nbsp;billion in earnings while still playing|url=https://www.cbssports.com/nba/news/lebron-james-becomes-first-player-in-nba-history-to-make-1-billion-in-earnings-while-still-playing/|work=CBS Sports|date=July 22, 2021|access-date=July 24, 2021|archive-date=July 25, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210725082200/https://www.cbssports.com/nba/news/lebron-james-becomes-first-player-in-nba-history-to-make-1-billion-in-earnings-while-still-playing/|url-status=live}}</ref> James has been listed by ''[[Forbes]]'' as one of the [[Forbes list of the world's highest-paid athletes|world's ten highest-paid athletes]] fifteen times. He was among ''[[Time (magazine)|Time]]''{{'}}s [[Time 100|100 most influential people in the world]] in 2005, 2013, 2017, and 2019—the most selections for a professional athlete.

