'''Kyrie Andrew Irving''' ({{IPAc-en|ˈ|k|aɪ|r|i}} {{respelling|KY|ree}}; {{langx|lkt|Ȟéla|italic=no}}, {{literal translation|Little Mountain}}; born March 23, 1992) is an <!--***NOTE*** Please see [[Talk:Kyrie_Irving#Nationality]] for prior consensus to not list Australian ***NOTE***-->American<!--***NOTE*** Please see [[Talk:Kyrie_Irving#Nationality]] for prior consensus to not list Australian ***NOTE***--> professional [[basketball]] player for the [[Dallas Mavericks]] of the [[National Basketball Association]] (NBA). He was named the [[NBA Rookie of the Year|Rookie of the Year]] after being selected by the [[Cleveland Cavaliers]] with the [[List of first overall NBA draft picks|first overall pick]] in the [[2011 NBA draft]]. A nine-time [[NBA All-Star|All-Star]] and three-time member of the [[All-NBA Team]], Irving won an [[NBA championship]] with the Cavaliers in 2016.

Irving played one year of [[college basketball]] for the [[Duke Blue Devils men's basketball|Duke Blue Devils]] before joining the Cavaliers in 2011. He won the [[NBA All-Star Game Kobe Bryant Most Valuable Player Award|Most Valuable Player]] (MVP) award for the [[2014 NBA All-Star Game|2014 All-Star Game]]. In the [[2016 NBA Finals]], Irving made the championship-winning [[three-point field goal|three-pointer]] to complete the Cavaliers' historic comeback over the [[Golden State Warriors]]. After another Finals appearance in 2017, Irving requested a trade and was dealt to the [[Boston Celtics]]. He played with the Celtics for two seasons, after which Irving signed with the [[Brooklyn Nets]] as a [[free agent]] in 2019. After four seasons with the Nets, he requested a trade and was dealt to the Dallas Mavericks in 2023, with whom Irving reached his fourth NBA Finals in [[2024 NBA Finals|2024]]. He has also played for the [[United States men's national basketball team|United States national team]], with which Irving won gold at the [[2014 FIBA Basketball World Cup]] and the [[Basketball at the 2016 Summer Olympics – Men's tournament|2016 Summer Olympics]]. In February 2020, he was elected as one of the seven vice-presidents of the [[National Basketball Players Association]], replacing [[Pau Gasol]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=February 17, 2020 |title=Kyrie replaces Pau as a VP of players' union |url=https://www.espn.com/nba/story/_/id/28724791/kyrie-irving-replaces-pau-gasol-vp-players-union |access-date=July 8, 2021 |publisher=ESPN |archive-date=May 15, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210515062835/https://www.espn.com/nba/story/_/id/28724791/kyrie-irving-replaces-pau-gasol-vp-players-union |url-status=live}}</ref> Irving's decision not to get [[COVID-19 vaccine|vaccinated for COVID-19]] led to him missing the majority of the [[2021–2022 NBA season]].

Throughout his career, Irving has promoted numerous [[conspiracy theories]]. Some of these conspiracies, including tweeting a link to the film ''[[Hebrews to Negroes: Wake Up Black America]]'' (2018), have been characterized as [[Antisemitism|antisemitic]]. For a time prior, Irving promoted a theory that [[Modern flat Earth beliefs|the Earth is flat]], which he later recanted.<ref>{{cite web|last=Blackburn|first=Pete|date=October 1, 2018|url=https://www.cbssports.com/nba/news/kyrie-irving-apologizes-for-saying-earth-is-flat-claims-he-was-into-conspiracies/|title=Kyrie Irving apologizes for saying Earth is flat, claims he was 'into conspiracies'|website=CBS Sports|access-date=May 17, 2023}}</ref>

Irving has written, directed, and acted in a number of advertisements as "Uncle Drew", which became [[Uncle Drew|a feature film]] in 2018. He has starred as himself in ''[[Kickin' It]]'' (2012) and has done [[voice acting|voicework]] in ''[[We Bare Bears]]'' (2016) and ''[[Family Guy]]'' (2018).
